     3	## What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied `MPY` reference semantics, the `prime_length` closure in
     6	`spec.k`, when called with any semantic string `str(CS)` whose structural
     7	length is nonnegative, returns `primeNat(isLen(CS))`. The closure body is the
     8	constructor-for-constructor translation of `solution.py`; lookup, argument
     9	evaluation, `len`, integer operations, the loop, assignments, return, and frame
    10	pop all execute using the unmodified reference rules.
    11	
    12	This is a partial-correctness result: every terminating execution in the stated
    13	domain returns the specified Boolean. The K claim does not assert termination.
    14	
    15	`primeNat(N)` is true exactly when `N >= 2` and none of the candidates
    16	`2, 3, ..., N - 1` divides `N`. That is the standard characterization of a
    17	prime natural number. Therefore the returned Boolean says exactly whether the
    18	input string's length is prime.
    19	
    20	## Formal claim
    21	
    22	The entry claim is `SPEC.prime-length`:
